Mobility changes the relationship between a child and a family dog: parents describe lunging, a bleeding facial scratch, growling and nipping as babies crawl or approach food. The children are seven months, nearly one year and fourteen months; another nearly-one-year-old’s parent focuses on dirty floors, sensory distress and conflict over keeping the dog. The parents describe separation, training, a muzzle, temporary care elsewhere or potential rehoming, with attachment and outside judgement making decisions painful.

- "As far as we’d seen, she had always been good with children but once our son started to become mobile, she was clearly stressed and started to display a few signs of aggression. I have been watching her closely, knowing that if it happened again, we would unfortunately have to find another home for her. Yesterday she lunged towards my 7 month old, trying to bite him. Luckily, I was right there and caught her by the collar before she could do anything but it was such a terrifying moment and who knows what could have happened had I not been there." [sr044-0002]
- "Twice now he has come ovee and touched him and mt dog has gotten upset. The first time he growled and showed teeth in his face and walked away. The second time, he did the same except his tooth made contact with his face and he bled. It was a small scratch but it horrified me." [sr113-0141]
- "My heart was pounding and I was afraid my son would be scared or that the dog would go for him after. It’s not the first time the dog has nipped my husband aggressively during baths or drying him afterwards. My son will be eating crackers and the dog will go and take it out of his hands and I’m just so afraid now. I always had this fear that he would hurt us or my son one day and that fear is becoming stronger as my son becomes more and more mobile and curious." [sr117-0149]
- "Our baby is now almost 1 year old and crawling. We live in a 2-bedroom apartment in NYC, and the streets here are filthy. The dog walks outside where people and animals use the bathroom, and my partner doesn't clean his paws when they come inside. The shedding, dirt, and smell fill me with pure rage because my baby is crawling on those same floors. I can't handle the hygiene aspect of it." [sr091-0168]

**But:** Love and attachment persist alongside the fear. "I feel like I know what we should do,  but I also feel heartbroken because despite his awful behavior and aggressiveness, I still love him…… please don’t judge me. This is SO hard and I know that my son’s safety needs to come first, but I can’t imagine my life without my dog either. He was our first dog together and we have so many good memories. This is so hard." [sr117-0150]

Posts: 1tr6eht 1vvhrwi 1wlnq3b 1wun5f4

### The parent wants room for their own needs while caring for a demanding child.

14 of 213 posts.

These parents want a break, bodily space, exercise, sleep, ordinary housework or a sense of their old independence while caring for a child. Known stages range from seven weeks postpartum to a twenty-two-month-old, and one post concerns a newborn arriving while an older child’s sleep deteriorates. Posts include regret about parenthood, shame after frustration, loss of identity, managing work with a newly walking baby, and a practical deodorant purchase when showering is difficult. They also describe postpartum body odour and a painful return of periods amid breastfeeding and lost sleep.

- "I don’t know if i’m alone in this feeling but recently i wish i wasn’t a mother. I love my son to bits and would do anything for him but miss the freedom, and not having to worry 24/7 about my baby. I don’t like how i have so many responsibilities now." [sr113-0112]
- "At some point I woke up from a nightmare about him, I was hot, and I just broke down crying. I found myself grieving my old life — my independence, my mental space, the freedom of not having this constant responsibility. The physical weight of always being needed, having to do contact naps not knowing when it will end, always being "on"." [sr072-0060]
- "Last night however at 3am…I just lost it. Not at the baby, but I came out his room and woke my partner up in tears basically saying “I can’t do this! I need to f\*cking sleep…and I need to sleep without him touching me or kicking me, or having to hold him!! I don’t understand why he won’t sleep!!! I know it’s not his fault bit why won’t he f\*cking sleep I’ve done everything!”" [sr012-0181]
- "I use my son's nap to either get things done, or rest and recharge. With a second on the way, and knowing his nap length will just continue to decrease over time, it makes me anxious thinking about how my time to myself during the day will gradually just dissappear. Thanks for any advice or input!" [sr090-0126]
- "I just want to breathe. I want to figure out how to cope with this new life the best that I can. I am grieving my old life. I am grieving my marriage. I am grieving the old me." [sr088-0076]
- "My mom suggested clinical strength deodorant so I went to the store to get some. Let me tell you… best ten dollars I’ve spent." [sr029-0075]
- "And no one told me it would be painful to use tampons again!!? Just me!? I have had some scar tissue from my episiotomy and still having pain while doing the deed, but I didn’t even think about tampons… both in and out is torture, and pads give me too much of a familiar feeling to newly postpartum. Ugh." [sr054-0017]

**But:** One parent finds a solo week with one child unexpectedly spacious, even with a sleep regression. "And it reminded me how much down time there is with just 1 kid. The house has been clean the whole time, I’ve made some awesome meals, worked out daily. He even had a sleep regression this week, and it still felt like I had too much time. Anyone else experience this?" [sr064-0049]

