### The parent asks how everyday teaching and relationships shape the child.

12 of 213 posts.

These posts ask about parenting versus genetics, both parents’ contribution to development, modelling behaviour, literacy, healthy habits and how to explain limits or death. Ages are often unknown; examples include children around two years and a two- and four-year-old browsing books, plus an older sibling’s rejection of a baby. One poster recalls wanting to harm peers in childhood while asking about empathy and traits they call sociopathic; this is a personal account, not a diagnosis. A self-promotional post by an app’s creator reports toddlers understanding a visual travel-time display; it is labelled here as promotion rather than independent buyer evidence.

- "No I’ve read literal papers on sociopathic traits being common in toddlers but they grow out of it. I was definitely one of them. I had a thing until I was maybe 7 years old where I really wanted to hurt other peers or put peers in situations where they could get hurt bc I was curious what would happen. But I’ve always had such a deep and profound care for animals that I’m pretty sure that’s why my parents weren’t too concerned 😅" [sr001-0196]
- "I'm looking for real, quality research on the impact of both mom and dads presence and nurturing on the infants brain and development. Thanks!" [sr011-0211]
- "But then I was worried maybe that didn’t make sense to him? I also worry about him thinking his loved ones will go away forever. Like, yes…eventually they will, but I don’t want it to worry him like it’s an impending event. I’m also struggling because I do have a Christian worldview that I would like to impart, but it almost feels disingenuous to skip right to people going to heaven and not impart some kind of naturalistic understanding first. I don’t want him to think of death as just a waiting room where people hang out for a while…I feel like that has to come later, after they understand the gravity of death. Or maybe I’m over-thinking." [sr035-0013]
- "Does dragging your finger (as the adult) under words while reading to a child help at all with their literacy development?" [sr037-0050]
- "My kids kept asking "are we there yet?" because they have zero concept of time. So I made a simple app: one big screen, red at the top, green filling up from the bottom as we drive closer." [sr046-0084]
- "*they notice how we speak to other people, they notice how we handle frustration even when we think they are not paying attention, it made me realize that the example we set every day is often more powerful than any lesson we try to teach, that was a pretty humbling realization for me.*" [sr054-0069]
- "I wonder what is going on in their brains when they do this? Presumably they are only looking at the pictures because they can't read yet. How is this beneficial over real life experiences - Just like how in person interaction is better than leaning from the TV?" [sr131-0097]

**But:** A plausible teaching technique is not always welcomed by children. "[…] anecdotally, I've had kids ask me to stop using my finger when reading to them because it gets in the way of the pictures." [sr037-0052]

