## Pattern 0, share 03: yes posts

### The parent wants to wean the pacifier but worries about distress, readiness or losing good sleep.

3 of 31 posts.

These parents describe strong pacifier attachment at almost 3, 21 and 22 months. One has already cut the tip and seen missed naps and night waking; another has conflicting dental and pediatric advice, while the third anticipates guilt before starting.

- "The whole process has been incredibly stressful and I'm worried that she's not ready yet." [sr008-0106]
- "Dentist wants it gone by 2 and said go cold turkey. Peds said poke holes in them or get binky weaning system." [sr072-0088]
- "She is very attached to her pacifier for comfort and will sometimes cry just for the pacifier." [sr124-0038]

**But:** The attempted weaning has reduced use, so the account does not show that nothing changed: "We continued to trim down the paci because she's continued asking for it and using it, although a lot less than before." [sr008-0105]

Posts: 1sjtgte 1v279p7 1wyfwr0

### The parent wants to understand or soothe a baby whose crying does not yield to the expected remedy.

4 of 31 posts.

At one month, eight weeks and around three months, parents describe colic remedies, nursing that no longer works as a universal comfort, or a song that does calm the baby. Another post, staged 4–6 months, jokes that unrelenting misery must precede an extraordinary developmental milestone. Both failed attempts and a reported success belong here; the song's scientific claim is the poster's claim.

- "With my first, the boob would always work if all else fails but this one couldn’t care less about anything I try." [sr017-0164]
- "I’ve tried the mylicon and prebiotics and nothing helps 😔." [sr050-0057]
- "Can't do anything to keep him happy." [sr108-0081]
- "Anyway, it totally works with my 3mo!!" [sr056-0011]

**But:** One parent explicitly separates comfort refusal from feeding difficulty: "Breastfeeding itself is fine, little one doesn’t have a tongue tie or any issues. She’s gaining weight rapidly and eats plenty every three hours, day and night." [sr017-0160]

Posts: 1svlk6y 1u0rd6q 1ucwdpz 1wgj0pl

### The parent needs relief from the physical demands of holding and settling a baby.

4 of 31 posts.

Parents of five- and seven-week-olds describe almost continuous contact and wanting enough time to eat, use the bathroom or reset. A nine-week-old's flailing prompts a wish for a third hand, while carrying a 22-pound 6.5-month-old hurts another parent's back. Attempts include shared shifts, carriers, walking and rocking, and reflux assessment or treatment.

- "I’m currently 5 weeks postpartum, and my baby needs 24-hour contact." [sr111-0053]
- "I don’t want to quit BF, just want baby to give me like 15-25 minutes away from him during the day or a crib nap so I can nap." [sr083-0029]
- "Other parents: how did you manage caring for your huge baby before they could crawl or walk? My back hurts!" [sr057-0091]
- "I wish I had 3rd hand so I can sooth him or pat him gently on face." [sr065-0043]

**But:** Practical support is present in one account, even though it does not remove the feeding constraint: "I have a great helpful husband and we take shifts sleeping with baby overnight but I’m EBF" [sr083-0028]

Posts: 1uiw8zb 1uo6osf 1vcvlay 1wm1npe

### The parent wants to help a toddler tolerate separation after a change in home or parental presence.

2 of 31 posts.

One one-year-old becomes clingy after a move from a house to an apartment; another almost-two-year-old panics when a military father leaves sight between repeated absences. The parents try familiar surroundings, toys, advance explanations and reassurance about brief departures. They want the child to adjust without distress whenever an adult moves away.

- "He’ll let me put him down, but he won’t let me walk no more than a few feet away from him unless he has toys to distract him" [sr035-0109]
- "My husband can’t even go to the bathroom without our son needing to be at his feet or he’s a wreck." [sr086-0093]
- "We try to prepare him, the week or few days leading up to him leaving we start to tell him that daddy’s leaving soon for a little bit" [sr086-0094]

**But:** The separation difficulty is not absolute in the moving-home account: "unless he has toys to distract him" [sr035-0109]

Posts: 1ti1ync 1vjeld6

### The parent wants new movement skills to be manageable and safe when they cannot intervene immediately.

3 of 31 posts.

At five months, one parent asks how soon inchworming becomes crawling and what babyproofing gets missed; another wants to teach tummy-to-back rolling before daycare staff can respond. At eight months, pulling to stand brings repeated tumbles even inside a padded playpen. The concern is how to get anything done, or accept a delay in help, as movement changes what supervision requires.

- "How are other 8 month old parents getting anything done?!" [sr102-0032]
- "What part of baby proofing did you initially miss/forget to consider? What tips or life hacks do you have for me?" [sr039-0060]
- "I thought maybe if I could teach him to flip tummy to back, I would feel better if it took a teacher a few min to get to him." [sr121-0004]

**But:** The daycare concern changes this parent's wish to accelerate a skill; the parent otherwise says waiting would be acceptable: "I’d be fine with waiting but he is now in daycare (started today)" [sr121-0003]

Posts: 1tn6fmy 1w4q7i5 1wv6cx8

### The parent wants to understand why a toddler refuses particular foods or eats differently in different places.

3 of 31 posts.

A one-year-old accepts a narrowed diet at home after illness but seems okay at daycare. At 21–22 months, another child eats broadly at home but unpredictably in restaurants, while another resists cold foods even with playful modelling. The posts ask how to regain acceptance or whether others see the same selectivity.

- "He was a pretty good eater before, but now he is only eating crackers and reverting back to purées at home. But only at home!" [sr038-0158]
- "He won't try new foods, and he'll even sometimes reject familiar foods." [sr082-0044]
- "he will MAYBE lick it if we make it so so silly and model it." [sr067-0084]

**But:** The restaurant account includes successful meals, so refusal is not consistent: "There's been a few times where he has done well at restaurants, but it feels like a coin flip." [sr082-0044]

Posts: 1tpqs61 1uwssu2 1vfkjta

### The parent wonders how the baby's interests or temperament will change as they grow.

2 of 31 posts.

One parent asks when a 15-month-old's enthusiasm for vehicles might lead to dinosaurs, including whether that phase is skipped. Another compares a quieter eight-month-old with an outgoing older sibling and asks what quiet babies become as toddlers. These are questions about individuality and its future, without a stated developmental delay.

- "I’m curious when did your kids start getting into dinosaurs? And how did that interest start and evolve?" [sr035-0123]
- "My second child is currently 8 months and seems like they’ve always had a quieter energy." [sr045-0046]
- "If you had a quieter baby, what kind of toddler did they become?" [sr045-0048]

**But:** Comparison is explicitly qualified by acceptance: "For the record, however they want to be is fine with me and I strive to give each the same love, care, and attention." [sr045-0047]

Posts: 1tiv356 1tstgx0

### The parent wants the child to grow up connected to the family's language or nonwhite heritage.

2 of 31 posts.

A parent of a very young mixed-race baby worries about rejection of nonwhite identity in a predominantly white city. A newborn's family wants bilingual English and Spanish development when only one parent is fluent in Spanish. Visits to a home country are planned conditionally, while the other poster says their own Spanish is not yet conversational.

- "I see a lot of young people completely reject their non white heritage and it worries me." [sr038-0119]
- "we do plan to take lots of trips to my home country if money and health allows." [sr038-0120]
- "How do we teach a child Spanish when only one parent speaks it, since I’m nowhere near fluent enough to hold much of a conversation yet." [sr065-0077]

**But:** The bilingual parent is not described as the only fluent person in the extended family: "he and his entire family are fluently bilingual (Spanish and English)." [sr065-0077]

Posts: 1to9g08 1uq8fwe

### The parent is unprepared for the practical changes that come with a baby's first teeth.

2 of 31 posts.

One parent of a nine-month-old with almost six teeth is distressed by the grinding sound. Another asks how to start brushing the first two teeth at almost five months, including gums, duration and a possible song. One post shares an unpleasant surprise; the other seeks a usable daily routine.

- "Why did no one tell me my baby might grind her teeth when teething…? Absolutely horrible sound." [sr053-0010]
- "I know I need to start brushing them but...how do I go about this?" [sr102-0123]
- "Should I just get it done by brushing these 2? Or brush all her gums too and make it take 2 minutes?" [sr102-0124]

**But:** nothing found against it.

Posts: 1u5j34r 1wa2aij

### The parent wants to know whether the baby's eye colour will change.

1 of 31 posts.

A parent of an almost-five-month-old with very blue eyes compares the baby's colour with both parents and extended family. The parent asks when others first saw a change and doubts predictions that the eyes will remain blue.

- "When did you first notice your baby’s eyes weren’t blue anymore?" [sr059-0071]
- "I have green eyes and my husband has brown, but my almost 5 month old has very blue eyes currently." [sr059-0072]
- "Everyone who sees her says “it definitely looks like she’s going to stay blue eyed”" [sr059-0072]

**But:** The family history includes blue eyes: "My parents both have blue eyes and both sets of my grandparents do as well." [sr059-0072]

Posts: 1ufb8fv

### The parent wants to understand a toddler's sign so they can respond when the child cries.

1 of 31 posts.

The parent of a 15-month-old describes an opening-and-closing fist with the thumb tucked in. The parent thinks the sign was learned at nursery but does not know what it means; the child repeats it while crying.

- "my baby (15M) is doing sign language I think they learn it at nursery but I don't know what it means" [sr095-0005]
- "the sign he's doing is like a fist with his thumb tucked in he's opening and closing it" [sr095-0005]
- "He keeps doing it and crying lol I'm clueless" [sr095-0006]

**But:** nothing found against it.

Posts: 1vpqyvq

### The parent wants happy toddler stories to make a hard first year feel worth getting through.

1 of 31 posts.

The parent of a ten-month-old says the first year has been hard and asks for positive experiences between one and two. Walks, play, laughter and shared discoveries are the moments the parent imagines and wants to anticipate.

- "Sometimes I genuinely feel like I’m going to lose my mind." [sr097-0021]
- "I’m holding on to the thought that better times are coming." [sr097-0022]
- "I imagine us going for walks, playing together, laughing, discovering things, and actually having fun together." [sr097-0022]

**But:** nothing found against it.

Posts: 1vx0zwr

### The parent wants to stop food being rubbed into the toddler's hair without taking away self-soothing.

1 of 31 posts.

This post is staged 18–24 months and describes hair rubbing that started before solids and continues during meals. Repeatedly washing food out is intensely irritating, but the parent does not want to remove what they see as the child's only self-soothing habit.

- "He STILL rubs his hands through his hair while eating and it makes me want to pull my own hair out." [sr113-0200]
- "I have to wash mashed up beans out of his hair for the THIRD time today." [sr113-0200]
- "He doesn’t suck his thumb so I don’t want to take away his only self soothing thing." [sr113-0202]

**But:** The habit is not confined to eating: "He twirls his hair when he’s sleepy or hungry, and he’s always rubbed his hands in his hair." [sr113-0199]

Posts: 1wnhecj

### The parent wants to know whether a frightening incident will leave the toddler with a lasting fear.

1 of 31 posts.

A 22-month-old screamed about a frog that was later found inside the child's pants. The parent calmed the child and released the frog together, but the child then avoided the bathroom and pants and repeated reassurance about no frog being there. The parent mixes laughter, self-reproach and questions about a new phobia.

- "She will not go in the bathroom (or put her pants on) because of the frog." [sr131-0012]
- "She keeps assuring herself “no frog in pants” and crying a little and takes everything in me to not laugh." [sr131-0013]
- "Will this be the start of a new phobia? Will she be able to go into the bathroom and not be scared of a frog in her pants?" [sr131-0013]

**But:** The child did calm before the frog was discovered, so distress was not uninterrupted: "She is calm and all is right in the world." [sr131-0011]

Posts: 1xaecmz

### The parent wants the father to be ready for developmental changes when he takes over parental leave.

1 of 31 posts.

A parent returning to work after leave describes a 13-week-old and the husband's upcoming 12 weeks of leave. The current wake-feed-play-sleep routine works, and bottle practice is underway, but nursing will no longer be available for daytime settling. The parent asks what else to anticipate over the coming months.

- "my husband is starting his 12 weeks of FMLA." [sr029-0099]
- "I think for my husband the biggest limitation will be the absence of a boob as it makes nap time very easy." [sr029-0100]
- "Outside of that, are there developmental type things or things that I’m not even thinking about that we can prepare for?" [sr029-0100]

**But:** The account starts from a functioning routine: "My LO and I have had a solid routine of wake, feed, play, sleep, repeat." [sr029-0100]

