## Pattern 0, share 03: near posts

### The parent wants to know when disrupted sleep will pass and how to get through it.

37 of 212 posts.

These posts describe formerly long stretches turning into repeated wakes, false starts, short naps or bedtime refusal, from three weeks through toddlerhood and one just-turned-two-year-old. Parents ask whether this is a regression, what causes it, how long it lasts and whether anything helps; some mainly want solidarity. Attempts range from routines and settling to medication, brief training attempts and bedsharing, with different results.

- "How long did your guys last? Did you feel this way? What did you do to help them out of it? Who did you speak to if you did?" [sr019-0095]
- "Baby’s longest stint in 6 weeks is an hour and a half but most of them have been 30-45 minutes." [sr018-0038]
- "I feel exhausted on a cellular level and need to see hope for when I can expect this to be over." [sr013-0199]
- "Im frustrated that I cant figure out why shes stopped being able to soothe herself, stopped being able to pull a blanket back on." [sr109-0125]
- "Is this just a phase or the 3-month sleep regression? How long did it last, and is there anything that actually helped?" [sr065-0051]
- "I am just someone who can become consumed and overwhelmed with too much data." [sr109-0029]

**But:** Not every account describes uniformly bad sleep across the day and night: "The only positive is that he sleeps brilliantly at night." [sr065-0049]

