### The parent says new movement skills keep waking the baby or turning bedtime into play.

9 of 212 posts.

Rolling, pulling up, cruising and pre-crawling rocking appear in accounts from about 4–5 months through almost one year. Parents describe babies rolling into an unwanted position, standing instead of lying down, or climbing during lengthy settling. They ask how to prevent the repeated wake-and-resettle cycle or whether mastering another movement will help.

- "She screams when she rolls. She hasn’t learned to roll from stomach to back." [sr088-0094]
- "And then I flip him and he does it again. And again. And again." [sr105-0092]
- "I basically need some guidance on how to not derail this great sleeper now that he’s learnt this new skill." [sr006-0101]
- "he will start rolling around , climbing our reclined bodies on the bed for more than 1 hour before he fell asleep." [sr118-0019]
- "So you're saying there's nothing I can do to help him learn this quicker?" [sr005-0172]

**But:** One parent specifically says the problem is settling, rather than the belly position once asleep: "He sleeps on his belly finger once he's asleep if he rolls in a deep sleep, but just during the falling asleep process it wakes him up." [sr105-0094]

