### The parent describes how caring for a child has affected their feelings or sense of self.

21 of 213 posts.

These posts describe exhaustion, identity loss, guilt, grief, compulsive checking or scrolling, regret, difficulty bonding, rage with fear of hurting a baby, suicidal thoughts and fear of death as children grow. Ages include two weeks postpartum, babies around three to ten months and toddlers, while some ages are unknown; experiences also include premature birth, hospital separation and miscarriage while already parenting. Some have sought medication, therapy, a psychiatrist, a doctor or a doula, or set a baby down and walked away; others ask for reassurance, encouragement or space to vent. The night-waking poem is a literary account, and the television scene mentioned in the bonding post is fictional, rather than independent testimony about another family.

- "Now here we are. Postpartum hit me like a bus. Panic attacks, anxiety about my loss of self and freedom, difficulty bonding with the baby, waves of regret about this choice." [sr013-0056]
- "I honestly wish that if i ended my life i could go back to before i had her and get an abortion." [sr050-0151]
- "The second wake up just filled me with rage. Like I had to set him down and walk away because I just wanted it to stop and my brain was saying if I didn’t walk away I might hurt him." [sr078-0051]
- "However I am terrified if I don't obsess, I will miss something." [sr059-0121]
- "My phone is the only thing that can hold my attention with this level of sleep deprivation. It sucks because I don’t want to be using my phone much around my baby. But the little dopamine I get from scrolling is hard to resist." [sr091-0039]
- "I had hit my point of breaking, i actually hated my kid and despised havingto watch him. I called my doctor called in a doula and I started going to therapy." [sr124-0052]
- "Ever since giving birth for the first time this year, death is on my mind a lot. Like I’m not suicidal but like I dread and actively fear the death of my parents, partner, people around me, myself." [sr134-0126]

**But:** Severe tiredness is not presented only as regret or rejection: the literary account ends with gratitude: "Every time the prayer is the same. For all the endless nights, and days between, for all the broken sleep and sleepless dreams, for all the ache and all the joy, thank you, God, for this gift, our boy." [sr051-0079]

