### The parent is trying to work out how much sleep their child needs and when to help them sleep.

18 of 213 posts.

These posts concern short or refused naps, early starts, split nights, unusually long or short wake windows, one- or two-nap transitions, and whether a quietly awake child needs help. Ages include four-month-olds, a six-month-old born at 30 weeks and toddlers up to 23 months. Parents have tried caps, longer windows, earlier or later bedtimes, sleep cues and different nap methods, while daycare, stimulation, outings and a late nap complicate the timetable. Some describe being unable to make a baby follow the recommended amounts even when night sleep or mood is good.

- "We‘ve trimmed her nap to 45 mins for weeks with bedtime at 8:30 and wake at 6:15, but she still has middle of the night wakings." [sr013-0089]
- "Everywhere I read says “transition to 1 nap and cap it at 1.5hrs”. Sure. I would. IF SHE SLEPT MORE THAN 30 MINUTES." [sr013-0116]
