### The parent weighs paid work against staying home and missing the child’s milestones.

3 of 213 posts.

One stay-at-home parent of a seven-month-old wants money and breaks, one parent facing the end of maternity leave fears missing development, and another considers reducing or leaving paid work after household earnings change. The latter two children’s ages are unspecified. The tradeoffs include career security, retirement savings, childcare costs, a route out of the house, time off and being present for milestones.

- "I’m a stay at home mom to a 7 month old baby and Ive been told that the reward is “having a bond with my child stronger then anyone else” or “experiencing every milestone with him” but I genuinely can’t help but feel this way when I get no money/breaks/time off since he was born… As a FTM, i’m realizing being a sahm is not for me 😣" [sr025-0164]
- "My maternity leave ends in one day. The thought of leaving my baby for 9 hours a day hurts so bad. Coworker just mentions how it hurts for a few weeks and then you learn to live with it. But the guilt is eating away at me. I feel like such a bad mom. I’ve been so stressed my milk supply has dropped to less than half of what it was two weeks ago. Which just adds to the guilt. And if I quit I set my family back financially, my career will take a hit, not saving for retirement, and who knows when I’d be able to secure another job as the market for career is saturated and competitive. I have a good job. But am I going to regret missing my baby grow and develop?" [sr052-0066]
- "The cons to me working: […] \-potentially missing out on babys milestones (I’ve missed a couple firsts since going back to work) […] \-feeling like I can’t get a break - if I stay at home I could ask my mom to watch my baby every so often while I go out with friends, or I could just come home and clean, watch a show. Whatever. When I work I feel bad asking my mom to babysit an extra day and having an extra day away from my baby. […] \-Having to work around my mom and sisters schedule. My mom watches my sisters two kids sometimes too. My sister is also at my mom’s house a lot with her babies and she can get mad if my mom is busy when she needs her. I’ve had to call off/rearrange my schedule a lot because of them and thats with my mom only watching her once a week. […] \-Paying my mom. I obviously would pay my mom to watch my baby, but even with her being super duper cheap.. I’m making minimum wage and only working two days a week so I wouldn’t be bringing much home really." [sr083-0049]

**But:** Work can also supply desired time outside the house and personal income. "I have the option to stay at home with my baby again, but my mom has also agreed to watch my baby a couple days a week just so I can continue to get out of the house and make myself a little extra money." [sr083-0047]

