## Pattern 0, share 03: yes posts

### The parent wants to wean the pacifier but worries about distress, readiness or losing good sleep.

3 of 31 posts.

These parents describe strong pacifier attachment at almost 3, 21 and 22 months. One has already cut the tip and seen missed naps and night waking; another has conflicting dental and pediatric advice, while the third anticipates guilt before starting.

- "The whole process has been incredibly stressful and I'm worried that she's not ready yet." [sr008-0106]
- "Dentist wants it gone by 2 and said go cold turkey. Peds said poke holes in them or get binky weaning system." [sr072-0088]
- "She is very attached to her pacifier for comfort and will sometimes cry just for the pacifier." [sr124-0038]

**But:** The attempted weaning has reduced use, so the account does not show that nothing changed: "We continued to trim down the paci because she's continued asking for it and using it, although a lot less than before." [sr008-0105]

Posts: 1sjtgte 1v279p7 1wyfwr0

### The parent wants to understand or soothe a baby whose crying does not yield to the expected remedy.

4 of 31 posts.

At one month, eight weeks and around three months, parents describe colic remedies, nursing that no longer works as a universal comfort, or a song that does calm the baby. Another post, staged 4–6 months, jokes that unrelenting misery must precede an extraordinary developmental milestone. Both failed attempts and a reported success belong here; the song's scientific claim is the poster's claim.

- "With my first, the boob would always work if all else fails but this one couldn’t care less about anything I try." [sr017-0164]
- "I’ve tried the mylicon and prebiotics and nothing helps 😔." [sr050-0057]
- "Can't do anything to keep him happy." [sr108-0081]
- "Anyway, it totally works with my 3mo!!" [sr056-0011]

**But:** One parent explicitly separates comfort refusal from feeding difficulty: "Breastfeeding itself is fine, little one doesn’t have a tongue tie or any issues. She’s gaining weight rapidly and eats plenty every three hours, day and night." [sr017-0160]

Posts: 1svlk6y 1u0rd6q 1ucwdpz 1wgj0pl

### The parent needs relief from the physical demands of holding and settling a baby.

4 of 31 posts.

Parents of five- and seven-week-olds describe almost continuous contact and wanting enough time to eat, use the bathroom or reset. A nine-week-old's flailing prompts a wish for a third hand, while carrying a 22-pound 6.5-month-old hurts another parent's back. Attempts include shared shifts, carriers, walking and rocking, and reflux assessment or treatment.

- "I’m currently 5 weeks postpartum, and my baby needs 24-hour contact." [sr111-0053]
- "I don’t want to quit BF, just want baby to give me like 15-25 minutes away from him during the day or a crib nap so I can nap." [sr083-0029]
- "Other parents: how did you manage caring for your huge baby before they could crawl or walk? My back hurts!" [sr057-0091]
- "I wish I had 3rd hand so I can sooth him or pat him gently on face." [sr065-0043]

**But:** Practical support is present in one account, even though it does not remove the feeding constraint: "I have a great helpful husband and we take shifts sleeping with baby overnight but I’m EBF" [sr083-0028]

Posts: 1uiw8zb 1uo6osf 1vcvlay 1wm1npe

### The parent wants to help a toddler tolerate separation after a change in home or parental presence.

2 of 31 posts.

One one-year-old becomes clingy after a move from a house to an apartment; another almost-two-year-old panics when a military father leaves sight between repeated absences. The parents try familiar surroundings, toys, advance explanations and reassurance about brief departures. They want the child to adjust without distress whenever an adult moves away.

- "He’ll let me put him down, but he won’t let me walk no more than a few feet away from him unless he has toys to distract him" [sr035-0109]
- "My husband can’t even go to the bathroom without our son needing to be at his feet or he’s a wreck." [sr086-0093]
- "We try to prepare him, the week or few days leading up to him leaving we start to tell him that daddy’s leaving soon for a little bit" [sr086-0094]

**But:** The separation difficulty is not absolute in the moving-home account: "unless he has toys to distract him" [sr035-0109]

Posts: 1ti1ync 1vjeld6

### The parent wants new movement skills to be manageable and safe when they cannot intervene immediately.

3 of 31 posts.

At five months, one parent asks how soon inchworming becomes crawling and what babyproofing gets missed; another wants to teach tummy-to-back rolling before daycare staff can respond. At eight months, pulling to stand brings repeated tumbles even inside a padded playpen. The concern is how to get anything done, or accept a delay in help, as movement changes what supervision requires.

- "How are other 8 month old parents getting anything done?!" [sr102-0032]
- "What part of baby proofing did you initially miss/forget to consider? What tips or life hacks do you have for me?" [sr039-0060]
- "I thought maybe if I could teach him to flip tummy to back, I would feel better if it took a teacher a few min to get to him." [sr121-0004]

**But:** The daycare concern changes this parent's wish to accelerate a skill; the parent otherwise says waiting would be acceptable: "I’d be fine with waiting but he is now in daycare (started today)" [sr121-0003]

Posts: 1tn6fmy 1w4q7i5 1wv6cx8

### The parent wants to understand why a toddler refuses particular foods or eats differently in different places.

3 of 31 posts.

A one-year-old accepts a narrowed diet at home after illness but seems okay at daycare. At 21–22 months, another child eats broadly at home but unpredictably in restaurants, while another resists cold foods even with playful modelling. The posts ask how to regain acceptance or whether others see the same selectivity.

- "He was a pretty good eater before, but now he is only eating crackers and reverting back to purées at home. But only at home!" [sr038-0158]
- "He won't try new foods, and he'll even sometimes reject familiar foods." [sr082-0044]
- "he will MAYBE lick it if we make it so so silly and model it." [sr067-0084]

**But:** The restaurant account includes successful meals, so refusal is not consistent: "There's been a few times where he has done well at restaurants, but it feels like a coin flip." [sr082-0044]

