Pattern 0, share 01: yes posts

### The parent wants outings to be possible when their child refuses a seat or carrier.

2 of 31 posts.

An almost-four-month-old cries in the car seat, stroller and carrier; a 20-month-old wants to walk or be held instead of sitting in a shopping cart. Both parents want a way to make getting out manageable, with one already having tried several carriers and two strollers.

- "My baby is now almost 4 months old and she hates the car seat, stroller or carrier." [sr007-0019]
- "We’ve tried different types of carriers and two types of strollers (bassinet attachment and the uppababy minu)." [sr007-0021]
- "It’s just hard to carry her when I’m shopping alone. Any advice to get her to like sitting in shopping carts again?" [sr011-0025]

**But:** The objection is to the equipment rather than to every way of going out: "She’s perfectly happy to walk or be held when we go grocery shopping." [sr011-0025]

Posts: 1sfwul5 1smdkf3

### The parent wants to help their baby tolerate unfamiliar outdoor textures.

1 of 31 posts.

A nine-and-a-half-month-old has spent much of life indoors, in a stroller or on a blanket, and now cries at sand and grass and dislikes pavement on the feet. The parent is exposing the baby slowly within the baby's limits and asks what has helped others.

- "She hates the feeling of pavement on her feet, does not like sand (cries) she is very hesitant about letting the grass touch her and does cry at that too." [sr021-0088]
- "I’m trying to expose her slowly and within her limits." [sr021-0088]
- "What are some things you’ve done to help your LO get through this?" [sr021-0089]

**But:** The parent does not treat texture sensitivity as specific to autism and describes a personal aversion too: "I don’t think it’s specific to autism" [sr021-0090] "Like mine is I can’t stand the sound of snow being walked on. It hurts my ears and makes my head crinkle inside (weird description but yea 😂)." [sr021-0091]

Posts: 1t3khm5

### The parent looks for ways to calm a crying baby.

4 of 31 posts.

Parents describe unexpectedly reliable songs as well as a three-week-old whose hours of evening crying leave a new father panicking and questioning his competence. Mario Kart humming, Nordic chants, punk rock and electronic music appear as things that worked; the children include newborns and an eight-week-old, while one infant's age is unknown. They ask for other parents' unusual fixes or advice about coping.

- "I’ve found that I panic and don’t know how to handle his outbursts." [sr040-0073]
- "I feel awful because I don’t want my wife to look at me as a failure, and she’ll never admit that to me, but there are times where I feel like I question whether or not I was ever cut out for this." [sr040-0073]
- "Hold him and hum the Mario Kart theme (he’s never seen it played or heard it)" [sr029-0109]
- "One day I had reached wits end and jokingly put on Psycho Killer by the Talking Heads and he almost immediately calmed down." [sr090-0002]
- "when he’s having a meltdown the only thing that calms him down is punk rock." [sr090-0003]
- "She's 8 weeks old, shuts up and listens for all 7m36s. I'm not even sick of it yet." [sr108-0025]

**But:** The father says feeding stops the crying, even though other attempts have not helped; the music posts report successes rather than an absence of soothing: "he does not stop crying unless we feed him.  Whether it’s a bottle or my wife’s breast." [sr040-0072]

Posts: 1tc0h4d 1toumpa 1vhuh77 1wb1ns4

### The parent wants to keep a newly mobile or experimenting child from getting hurt.

6 of 31 posts.

Parents of eight- and nine-month-olds worry about head impacts while babies crawl, sit, kneel, pull up and cruise, including whether repeated falls could cause brain problems. Another asks how to handle an eight-month-old putting the whole face in bath water, and an 18-month-old's parent asks how to teach going downstairs after the child walked off the top step. Attempts include a doctor call, carpet or mats, anxiety medication and ordering protective equipment; one parent says continuous watching is unrealistic alongside work and chores.

- "My 8 month daughter is hitting her back of the head everyday. Im so worried first it was tile now the treadmill & yesterday night my own head. I’m crying what if she gets some brain issue" [sr059-0086]
- "He fell over and hit his head yesterday and now I’m so nervous to take my eye off him for a second. That’s just not realistic since I have to do my job, clean, cook, etc. There isn’t someone who can sit and watch him 24/7." [sr068-0058]
- "I just ordered really thick and soft baby mats to put on my carpet, and I also ordered one of those backpack things that have  a pillow under his head if he falls. But I’m so nervous about head injuries." [sr068-0059]
- "What are we doing for the eight month old babies that are trying to stick their whole face in the water in the bathtub?" [sr033-0096]
- "I let her try it alone one day and she just walked off the top step like a cartoon character walking off a cliff." [sr075-0143]
- "Tell me stories about how you dealt with the stress , but also stories of your kids falling and being a-ok. I need a reminder of how resilient babies are." [sr061-0143]

**But:** A frightening impact is not always followed by continuing distress: "He cried hard for 20 seconds but seemed fine afterwards." [sr059-0013]

