### The parent wants to know who is supposed to check their baby's development.

1 of 31 posts.

The parent of a 5.5-month-old says the last developmental check was the GP appointment at eight weeks. Online accounts of regular checks prompt questions about whether this gap is normal and whether the GP is only for a problem; the parent is monitoring weight at home.

- "When I look at videos online people talk about regular checks on baby's development etc. But the only time they last checked was at 8 weeks by the GP?" [sr033-0124]
- "Is it normal no one checks on her after that?" [sr033-0124]
- "Would you inly go to the GP if there was an issue?" [sr033-0126]

**But:** They are already checking weight themselves, though this does not answer the question about developmental appointments: "We're monitoring her weight ourselves at home with a baby scale. She's 5.5m." [sr033-0125]

Posts: 1tiw9oi

### The parent wants help teaching their child a skill.

2 of 31 posts.

One parent wants a 4.5-month-old to lift the head and roll during tummy time despite hand-chewing and a first tooth. Another models waving to people in parks for a baby who has just turned seven months and can wave but has not connected it to greeting. The questions concern making practice effective and deciding whether to concentrate on one skill.

- "So I’m trying to encourage it again by doing lots of tummy time, but half the time he won’t lift his head because he’s too busy devouring his fingers." [sr038-0112]
- "Anyone have tips for how to encourage head lifting in tummy time?" [sr038-0113]
- "We go to parks (I’ll baby wear her facing forward) and I wave at everyone (that looks friendly enough to wave back) to try and teach her this social gesture." [sr115-0127]
- "Are you guys focusing on one skill at a time or throwing everything at them and seeing what sticks?" [sr115-0125]

**But:** These are not accounts of an absent ability: both parents describe things their baby can already do: "I know he can do it, and he has great head control and can sit assisted." [sr038-0113] "She knows how to wave. She will do it when she’s having a bottle to “practice” but hasn’t connected when to do it yet." [sr115-0127]

