Posts: 1tr7955

### The parent wants to feel loved while respecting their toddler's refusal of hugs.

1 of 31 posts.

A parent of a two-year-old describes knowing the child loves them but struggling to feel it because physical touch matters to them. They respect refused hugs and are proud of the child's boundaries, while asking whether modelling or celebrating affection would become manipulation.

- "Here’s my issue: my love language is physical touch. And even though I intellectually know she loves me, I find myself struggling to *feel* it. Which feels ridiculous to admit about a two-year-old who clearly adores me. But here I am." [sr052-0110]
- "I always respect that. Full stop. I’m not here to vent about a kid asserting boundaries. I genuinely believe she gets to decide who touches her and when," [sr052-0109]
- "I catch myself wondering: if I model hugs, make them fun, celebrate them when they happen, am I just sharing something I enjoy with her? Or am I subtly overriding something innate about who she is?" [sr052-0112]

**But:** The child already shows affection and excitement through other behaviour: "She runs to me at daycare pickup every single day, excited to show me her drawings and toys. She lights up. And I know she loves me." [sr052-0107]

Posts: 1ua9kx2

### The parent feels sad that their child's babyhood is passing.

3 of 31 posts.

Two parents describe sadness around a first birthday, including regret at not being mentally present in the early months because of family issues. Another becomes unexpectedly sad at 7.5 weeks when side-rolling means exchanging swaddles for sleep bags. Growth and milestones are described as welcome progress with an emotionally painful ending attached.

- "What hurts most is that I regret his early months as I feel like I wasn't mentally there to enjoy it (because of some serious family issues)." [sr049-0117]
- "I suddenly got extremely sad. Growing up seems to be a bittersweet thing." [sr088-0045]
- "My little man will continue to grow and progress, but he will never, be my little BABY boy anymore.." [sr088-0046]
- "Husband is fast asleep and I’m still up staring at him wondering where the teeny tiny newborn we brought home in August went." [sr123-0045]

**But:** The sadness can coexist with the practical transition going well: "On the plus side at least he’s sleeping okay in his new get up." [sr123-0046]

Posts: 1u3f5fh 1vkil7t 1wxt1dq

### The parent wants to reduce a comfort habit without replacing it with another problem.

6 of 31 posts.

Parents describe pacifier reduction at ten months, almost one year, 14 months and 22 months, and thumb-sucking questions at five and almost 21 months. They ask when and how to stop, whether it will disrupt sleep or affect teeth, and whether removing a pacifier could bring thumb-sucking instead. Some have already removed daytime or car use; illness and four teeth are said to have reversed an earlier reduction.

- "She got sick and started to cut 4 teeth at once and now she’s more attached to the pacifier than ever." [sr063-0038]
- "I can see that his top front four teeth are narrowing alot and he almost seems to be getting more attached recently?? Like ill say okay its time to go upstairs for bed and he will excitedly say BINKYYYYYY and im like oh my gosh holy crap how do I just get rid of this." [sr084-0050]
- "Should I cold turkey? Cut it? Binky fair? Build a bear??" [sr084-0051]
- "My almost 21 month old has been sucking his thumb since birth. It’s not just a “when he’s sleepy” thing, it’s like a compulsion/ addiction 😅 . His default is thumb in mouth, and it goes straight back in even if he’s eating in between bites." [sr102-0042]
- "I’ve been thinking lately whether to continue using it or not.. I read that a lot of parents would wean off around this age, but I’m worried that she’ll start sucking her thumb for comfort as she still suckle her hands during the day while playing." [sr120-0020]
- "We have taken it away during the day and recently away in the car, but she still relies on it for naps and bed." [sr126-0016]

**But:** Not every child is strongly attached, and the professionals mentioned in the thumb-sucking post are not yet concerned: "we’ve been using the pacifier since birth but she’s not overly attached to it, just to soothe her when needed like during long car rides." [sr120-0019] "The pediatrician and dentist aren’t concerned yet," [sr102-0043]

