### The parent wants the child to stay asleep after being put down.

11 of 213 posts.

Parents describe babies who wake as they reach a cot or shortly after a transfer, and older toddlers who now need prolonged holding or the parents’ bed. Ages range from an eleven-week-old to a child turning two, with an additional toddler staged eighteen to twenty-four months. Deep-sleep transfers, drowsy placement, bedside sleepers and bedsharing do not reliably solve the problem; one attempt prompted gagging, vomiting and a one-handed cleanup.

- "We cannot transfer him to the cot at all anymore. Even if he’s fully asleep, the second we lower him his eyes ping open. And on the rare occasion we do get him down, within seconds he starts flailing his arms and wakes himself up crying." [sr017-0064]
- "He is rolling so we can’t swaddle him, but we’ve tried everything else- aside from spending money we don’t have on random “must-have’s” that may or may not work. It takes forever to get him to fall asleep but when we finally get him to sleep, he wakes up immediately upon transfer. We’ve held him until he’s in deep sleep before transferring; he jolts awake immediately smiling and giggling. We’ve tried transferring him drowsy; smiles and giggles. We’ve tried bed sharing (something we swore we wouldn’t do, but we cannot sleep!!), and he wakes up every 30 minutes needing to be soothed back to sleep but if you don’t catch him in time- you guessed it; smiles and giggles. It would be super cute if we weren’t absolutely exhausted. It’s so frustrating." [sr026-0095]
- "I just want to give up and co-sleep to be honest. It's the only time he stays asleep anyway. I'm exhausted and need sleep so badly and my husband keeps pushing this bedside sleeper but the proximity doesn't make a lick of difference if I can't put the baby down at all. Fuck. Me." [sr035-0058]
- "My son has always been a decent sleeper but he turns 2 this week and for the last couple of weeks his sleep has just been super rough. He wakes up a couple hours into going to bed and then he refuses to transfer back into his crib. He often sleeps immediately after being picked up and then when we stand up from the rocker he arches his back and starts wiggling until he wakes himself up. There have been nights he has taken 2-3 hours to get back into his own bed. He is especially insistent on me holding him. He goes to sleep fine at bedtime." [sr065-0008]
