These parents compare wake windows, nap number, nap length and bedtime with early waking, false starts or split nights. The children range from four months to twenty-two months, and the practical pressures include work, babysitters and daycare’s fixed nap schedule. Some want a structure that fits the household, while others question whether standard expectations fit a lower-sleep-needs child; changes sometimes help and sometimes make no discernible difference.

- "I'm back to work now and he's between 2 babysitters for 4 hours everyday and I really need some more structure in his daily schedule. babysitters mostly just play with him and try to give him 1 or 2 naps while they're there." [sr011-0066]
- "The last 3 weeks she has woke 1-2 hourly. Waking up crying, either rocked or fed back to sleep. I am constantly doing nap maths, questioning is her day sleep too long, or is she overtired or under tired etc etc, should I change this or that. The list goes on. It is all consuming and tbh nothing seems to make much of a difference." [sr068-0046]
- "I tried to cut her nap down to 1.75 and 1.5. I also tried to add 10 min to her morning WW and 15 min to her afternoon WW. I’m unsure if any of this worked bc she still did one or the other (Split night or wake ups after putting down)." [sr084-0020]
- "I’ve tried following a five hour wake window before bedtime, as suggested by a sleep consultant, but a 6:30 PM bedtime seems so early and since he doesn’t sleep 12 hours typically, I don’t think this would work well for my DWT." [sr101-0062]
- "I have tested different bedtimes and found that when I put her to bed any time between 8.30pm and 10pm, then she will wake up every hour crying. But if I put her to sleep 10.30pm then she'll only wake up once to feed and get up for the day around 7 am. She also seems to sleep better on her side vs her back." [sr123-0090]
