Mobility changes the relationship between a child and a family dog: parents describe lunging, a bleeding facial scratch, growling and nipping as babies crawl or approach food. The children are seven months, nearly one year and fourteen months; another nearly-one-year-old’s parent focuses on dirty floors, sensory distress and conflict over keeping the dog. The parents describe separation, training, a muzzle, temporary care elsewhere or potential rehoming, with attachment and outside judgement making decisions painful.

- "As far as we’d seen, she had always been good with children but once our son started to become mobile, she was clearly stressed and started to display a few signs of aggression. I have been watching her closely, knowing that if it happened again, we would unfortunately have to find another home for her. Yesterday she lunged towards my 7 month old, trying to bite him. Luckily, I was right there and caught her by the collar before she could do anything but it was such a terrifying moment and who knows what could have happened had I not been there." [sr044-0002]
- "Twice now he has come ovee and touched him and mt dog has gotten upset. The first time he growled and showed teeth in his face and walked away. The second time, he did the same except his tooth made contact with his face and he bled. It was a small scratch but it horrified me." [sr113-0141]
- "My heart was pounding and I was afraid my son would be scared or that the dog would go for him after. It’s not the first time the dog has nipped my husband aggressively during baths or drying him afterwards. My son will be eating crackers and the dog will go and take it out of his hands and I’m just so afraid now. I always had this fear that he would hurt us or my son one day and that fear is becoming stronger as my son becomes more and more mobile and curious." [sr117-0149]
- "Our baby is now almost 1 year old and crawling. We live in a 2-bedroom apartment in NYC, and the streets here are filthy. The dog walks outside where people and animals use the bathroom, and my partner doesn't clean his paws when they come inside. The shedding, dirt, and smell fill me with pure rage because my baby is crawling on those same floors. I can't handle the hygiene aspect of it." [sr091-0168]

**But:** Love and attachment persist alongside the fear. "I feel like I know what we should do,  but I also feel heartbroken because despite his awful behavior and aggressiveness, I still love him…… please don’t judge me. This is SO hard and I know that my son’s safety needs to come first, but I can’t imagine my life without my dog either. He was our first dog together and we have so many good memories. This is so hard." [sr117-0150]

